6. Integration Points
Events Service (events-service)
- What it does: Receives audit event comments and stores them in the central event log.
- Why it matters: When a significant contract action occurs (amendment, payment
finalisation, lock/unlock), a comment is posted to the Events Service via
mergeEventComments. This provides a cross-system timeline of all activity on a site or
contract, visible to all teams across the Fuze platform — not just real estate users.
Bulk Service (bulkService / bulk-upload-service)
- What it does: Handles large-scale data operations that would be too heavyweight to run
inline in a web request.
- Why it matters: Updating 10,000 contracts one-at-a-time through the normal API would
time out. The Bulk Service accepts a batch of contract numbers and a change payload,
processes them offline, and reports results back. It also handles SQL script execution for
platform engineers during data migrations.
Document Service / NetDocs (documentService)
- What it does: Stores, retrieves, and manages the documents attached to lease
contracts.
- Why it matters: Lease agreements are legal documents that must be stored securely,
versioned, and auditable. The Document Service abstracts the underlying Documentum
content management platform. Fuze-netsites-service calls it to upload new contracts
(documentUpload), retrieve signed copies (getDocumentGet), and move files
between workflow stages (moveDocument).
Oracle Database
- What it holds: All master contract data, payment terms and history, site and parcel
records, user and role data, QC audit trails, system configuration, and SQL query
definitions (used by the DataCloud framework).
- Why it matters: Oracle is the authoritative persistent store for all business data. The
DataCloud framework dynamically loads SQL queries from the database at runtime,
meaning SQL can be tuned or corrected by DBAs without requiring a software
deployment.
Eureka Service Registry
- What it does: Acts as a phone book for microservices. Every Fuze microservice
registers itself with Eureka when it starts up, advertising its network address.
- Why it matters: Instead of hard-coding the IP addresses of the Events Service, Bulk
Service, and Document Service, fuze-netsites-service looks them up by name
from Eureka at runtime. If a downstream service is scaled up (more instances added),
Ribbon automatically load-balances calls across all healthy instances — with no
configuration change required.
